WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.134 --> 00:00:08.977
Many years ago the king of Greece had a beautiful wife named Helen.

2
00:00:09.657 --> 00:00:15.540
History remembers her as the face that launched a thousand ships because the most heroic war of the

3
00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:17.281
ancient world was fought over her.

4
00:00:18.122 --> 00:00:22.104
Greece's great rival was the kingdom of Troy, across the Aegean Sea.

5
00:00:23.905 --> 00:00:28.148
The king of Troy had a son named Paris who fell in love with Helen and stole her

6
00:00:28.208 --> 00:00:33.007
away. The Greeks demanded that Helen be returned, but the Trojans refused.

7
00:00:33.607 --> 00:00:36.689
So the Greek army set sail in a thousand ships to attack.

8
00:00:37.710 --> 00:00:42.693
The Greeks camped on the shores near Troy and battled the Trojans on the windy plains before the

9
00:00:42.733 --> 00:00:49.898
city. For ten long years, the Trojan War dragged on, but neither army could defeat the other.

10
00:00:50.779 --> 00:00:57.043
Finally the Greek king gathered his generals and said, "For ten years we have attacked the walls and

11
00:00:57.123 --> 00:01:01.193
gates of Troy, but our enemy has always beaten us back.

12
00:01:01.993 --> 00:01:04.834
We need a new strategy to help us conquer the city."

13
00:01:07.794 --> 00:01:10.995
A Greek general who was known for his cunning plans spoke up.

14
00:01:11.015 --> 00:01:12.376
"I have a plan!

15
00:01:13.056 --> 00:01:15.176
Let's pretend to sail home in our ships.

16
00:01:15.857 --> 00:01:19.318
The Trojans will think that we have given up and that the war is over."

17
00:01:19.338 --> 00:01:21.198
"And then what?"

18
00:01:22.078 --> 00:01:22.718
asked the king.

19
00:01:24.339 --> 00:01:28.340
The clever general explained the rest of his plan, and the king nodded his head.

20
00:01:29.152 --> 00:01:35.235
"Hmm, it is a daring strategy," he said, "but I believe it will work!"

21
00:01:36.495 --> 00:01:40.977
The next day the Greeks didn't attack, but set to work building a huge wooden horse.

22
00:01:43.118 --> 00:01:46.540
For weeks they labored by their ships and left the city alone.

23
00:01:47.260 --> 00:01:53.663
Finally when the towering horse was ready, a few brave soldiers climbed into its belly through a secret

24
00:01:53.723 --> 00:01:59.165
door. The statue was mounted on wheels, and the other soldiers rolled it up to the gates of

25
00:01:59.245 --> 00:02:04.508
Troy. They returned to their ships, and the Greek army sailed out of sight.

26
00:02:05.188 --> 00:02:09.411
When the Trojans saw what the Greeks had left, they weren't sure what to do.

27
00:02:10.311 --> 00:02:11.392
"Stay away from the horse.

28
00:02:11.812 --> 00:02:12.372
It's a trick!"

29
00:02:13.253 --> 00:02:14.333
Paris warned his father.

30
00:02:14.954 --> 00:02:19.796
After so many years of war, the Trojans couldn't believe that the Greeks had finally given up.

31
00:02:19.816 --> 00:02:24.639
"Hmm, you may be right," said the Trojan king.

32
00:02:24.659 --> 00:02:26.580
"We must be cautious."

33
00:02:27.404 --> 00:02:31.288
The huge horse stood outside the city gates while the soldiers inside waited.

34
00:02:32.169 --> 00:02:37.153
As the Trojans puzzled over what to do, a Trojan soldier brought a prisoner in front of the

35
00:02:37.193 --> 00:02:42.238
king. "Your Majesty," he said, "I caught this Greek near the city gates.

36
00:02:42.718 --> 00:02:43.819
He must be a spy.

37
00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:45.361
He should be put to death."

38
00:02:45.381 --> 00:02:49.024
"Oh no, spare my life, please!"

39
00:02:49.605 --> 00:02:50.245
begged the Greek.

40
00:02:50.946 --> 00:02:52.808
"I will tell you everything you want to know."

41
00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:58.182
The king agreed and asked him why the Greeks had left behind the enormous wooden statue.

42
00:02:58.202 --> 00:03:03.824
"Our army was exhausted," the Greek explained, "and wanted to retreat safely.

43
00:03:04.424 --> 00:03:08.466
They decided to offer you a great war trophy so that you would not pursue them.

44
00:03:09.026 --> 00:03:09.827
That's all I know."

45
00:03:09.847 --> 00:03:13.508
"So it is a war trophy then?

46
00:03:14.108 --> 00:03:16.869
Ha-ha-ha! The Greeks have given up!

47
00:03:17.950 --> 00:03:19.690
Bring the wooden horse inside!"

48
00:03:20.431 --> 00:03:21.111
ordered the king.

49
00:03:21.131 --> 00:03:23.172
"We have won the war!"

50
00:03:24.076 --> 00:03:30.158
the people of Troy declared, and both the soldiers and citizens began to celebrate as the wooden horse

51
00:03:30.218 --> 00:03:31.518
was dragged into the city.

52
00:03:32.158 --> 00:03:37.920
It was so wonderfully made that no one noticed the secret door that hid the Greek soldiers inside.

53
00:03:39.660 --> 00:03:44.582
No one noticed, as well, the captured Greek smiling as the Trojan soldiers got drunk.

54
00:03:45.242 --> 00:03:46.722
He was a spy, after all.

55
00:03:47.543 --> 00:03:51.804
All around him, the citizens and soldiers of Troy drank and sang late into the night.

56
00:03:52.628 --> 00:03:58.271
When they had all fallen asleep, the Greek spy quietly approached the horse and opened the hidden door

57
00:03:58.331 --> 00:03:58.931
in its belly.

58
00:03:59.632 --> 00:04:00.973
The Greek soldiers climbed out.

59
00:04:03.014 --> 00:04:09.077
They approached the drunken guards half-asleep by the city gates, quickly overpowered them, and pushed the gates open.

60
00:04:09.837 --> 00:04:14.840
The Greek ships had only pretended to sail away and had returned with the Greek army during the

61
00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:19.623
night. Greek soldiers poured through the gates and lit fires all over the city.

62
00:04:19.643 --> 00:04:21.944
"Fire! Fire!"

63
00:04:22.476 --> 00:04:24.818
the Trojans shouted, waking in surprise.

64
00:04:24.838 --> 00:04:31.184
"No!" they cried when they realized it was a surprise attack.

65
00:04:32.004 --> 00:04:32.805
Some ran away.

66
00:04:33.185 --> 00:04:35.567
Some fought bravely, but it was too late.

67
00:04:36.348 --> 00:04:38.410
The Greeks had finally conquered Troy.

68
00:04:39.271 --> 00:04:43.794
The story of the Trojan War belongs to the myths and legends of the ancient Greeks.

69
00:04:44.855 --> 00:04:49.920
It was written by the legendary Greek poet Homer, who lived almost three thousand years ago.

70
00:04:51.868 --> 00:04:57.671
Little is known about him, except that he was blind, and many scholars debate whether he really existed.

71
00:04:58.552 --> 00:05:05.916
Many doubted too that the city of Troy ever existed until, in the late nineteenth century, archaeologists discovered

72
00:05:06.076 --> 00:05:09.838
an ancient city from the time of Homer on the Aegean coast.

73
00:05:11.799 --> 00:05:18.283
A great battle may have been fought there, and who knows, perhaps it was destroyed forever by the

74
00:05:18.363 --> 00:05:20.024
trick of a huge wooden horse.
